  "notes": "Frame: contemporary United States (mid-2020s), broadly applicable to Canada/UK/AU; where requirements vary by state, the attribute is TRUE if it holds in a clear majority of states. Conventions applied consistently: (1) 'bachelor's degree or higher' means a four-year academic degree is the standard legal or de-facto entry requirement -- associate-degree and certificate routes count as vocational (attribute 8), not as a degree, so registered nurse, dental hygienist, radiologic technologist and paralegal are FALSE on degree and TRUE on vocational training; commercial airline pilot is FALSE on degree because the FAA requires certificates, not a degree. (2) 'licence or mandatory professional credential' covers state licensure (nursing, cosmetology, teaching), federal certificates (FAA ATP, CDL, NCCCO crane certification), POST/fire certification, securities and real estate registration, and commercial fishing permits. Voluntary credentials (AWS welding, ASE mechanics, ISA arborist) are FALSE. The actuarial fellowship is treated as FALSE (a professional exam credential, not a licence to practise). (3) 'apprenticeship / vocational / supervised placement' deliberately overlaps with degrees in two cases -- architect (AXP supervised experience) and clinical psychologist (supervised licensure hours) -- so the two education attributes are not mutually exclusive. (4) 'direct care of people' means personal responsibility for health, bodily needs, or physical safety; teaching and transport are excluded (teacher, bus driver FALSE), public-safety roles included (police, firefighter, security guard TRUE). (5) 'handling money or financial risk' is limited to occupations where it is a defined core duty (cash handling, fiduciary duty, pricing risk, or bearing business/commodity risk); incidental invoicing by self-employed tradespeople is FALSE. (6) 'self-employed or independent contractor' means a substantial share of practitioners (roughly >15%) work that way per BLS-style employment data; it is not the complement of 'salaried' -- attorneys, CPAs and architects are TRUE on both. (7) 'personal legal or professional liability' means the individual, not only the employer, is answerable for errors: malpractice exposure, fiduciary duty, personal sign-off on permitted or safety-critical work, or a revocable personal certificate. News reporter is TRUE (personal defamation exposure) without holding a licence, so liability does not reduce to licensure. (8) 'salaried' excludes hourly, per-mile and commission pay: airline pilots (flight-hour pay), police and firefighters (FLSA hourly with overtime), paralegals (non-exempt) and real estate agents (commission) are FALSE. Judgement calls worth flagging: plumbers are TRUE on shift/on-call (24-hour emergency service) while electricians are FALSE; anesthesiologists are TRUE on self-employment (locum and physician-group contracting); software engineers are TRUE on on-call (production rotations); veterinarians are FALSE on direct care of people (animal patients, human clients). Residual attribute implication: 'direct care of people' entails 'face-to-face interaction' across the whole object set -- that is a genuine semantic entailment of the domain, not an artefact, and it is the only one left; a 'degree implies salaried' artefact was removed by including the freelance translator. An 'involves writing code or software' attribute was tested and dropped: on any realistic occupation sample its density is under 0.10, far below the 0.15 floor, so it was replaced by the self-employment attribute.",
